explanations.E0026:
# E0026: name taken by a built-in

The names `i64`, `u8`, `f64`, `bool`, `char` and `string` belong to the built-in types. A class, a function, an enum or a constant cannot take one. A variant cannot be called `None`, which is a keyword.

The names `Option`, `Result`, `Some`, `Ok` and `Err` are built in too: no class, function, enum, constant, variant, local or parameter can take one. A field or a method can, since it is always reached through a value.

## Why

A type name stands where a type is expected, and `u8(...)`, `i64(...)`, `f64(...)` and `char(...)` convert a value. A class, a function or a constant with one of these names would make the same word mean two things. `Option` and `Result` name types, and `Some(x)`, `Ok(x)` and `Err(e)` build their values, so they are kept for them.

## Broken

```sisu
class u8 {
    n: i64
}

fn main() {
    print(1)
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
class Byte {
    n: i64
}

fn main() {
    let b = Byte(n: 1)
    print(b.n)
}
```

explanations.E0116:
# E0116: local named like an enum

A local, a parameter or a binding cannot take the name of an enum: `let Shape = 1` is an error when `Shape` is an enum.

## Why

`Shape.Circle` builds a variant of the enum `Shape`. If a local could take the name `Shape`, the same text could mean a method call on that local, and a reader would have to look for the binding to know which. A rule that rejects the name now can be relaxed later; a shadowing rule cannot be taken back. There is no fix, since the new name is yours to choose.

## Broken

```sisu
enum Shape {
    Dot
}

fn main() {
    let Shape = Shape.Dot
    print(Shape == Shape.Dot)
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
enum Shape {
    Dot
}

fn main() {
    let shape = Shape.Dot
    print(shape == Shape.Dot)
}
```
